From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: My Home Town

There may not be a lot more that can be productively said about this,
but one point I think is worth keeping in mind.  D. Owen Rowley writes:

> Well, i don't know what news sources the rest of you are watching, but 
> I've seen quite a lot of focus on other violent episodes in this groups past,
> and a few stories about how these guys threaten disaffected  members with 
> execution if they try to leave or get their relatives out.
> 
> If nothing else, it should be clear in hindsight that these people are not
> a benign movement of holy rollers who want to be left alone, these people
> aren't hobby gun folk or constitutional scholars.

    [ ... ]

> Thats what I'm saying, there do seem to me to be some legitimate reasons to 
> question their activities.
> I don't think that the usage of the word *just* above, really fits BTW.
> 
> The davidians show no evidence that they care about any other concerns but 
> their own. They do show evidence of sociopathic behavior.

presenting an argument that the Davidians very likely were not just
harmless wackos, but potentially dangerous neighbors, and may in fact
have committed crimes that in our current legal system would justify
even the use of armed force on the part of law enforcement.   This line
of thinking, as others have noted, has been subtly and not-so-subtly
promoted by the media and the authorities.
     OK, this may very well be true.  In fact, I expect it's probably
true, and for the sake of argument let's stipulate that indubitable
crimes like child abuse and molestation really were occurring, and
that local authorities had a responsibility to do something about it.
     But it wasn't the local authorities that were involved.  It was
the Bureau of *Alcohol*, *Tobacco*, and *Firearms*.  This agency has
no jurisdiction in matters of kidnapping, rape, child abuse, murder,
or any such crimes that may have been committed in the Davidians 
compound.  (In fact one can argue, from a point of view that is far short
of anarchism or radical libertarianism, that the BATF, like the DEA, is
a law enforcement agency that has *no* real crimes in its purview, and
thus no legitimate claim to any support or sympathy from honest citizens).
So nothing that we might believe about such crimes being actually or
potentially committed offers any justification for the raid, and
evidence as to the undesirability or outright criminality of the group is
not relevant to the actual issue. 

