I know it's already been pointed out that the message was forwarded by
an ordinary subscriber and that the White House didn't send the message
directly.

However, on a related note, if people are worried about government
interception of this mailing list, they shouldn't be worried about
anything as gross as an account at the White House. To obvious. If the
executive branch is actually interested in our discussions, it would be
a victory, not a defeat. (It would also be a very cold day in hell.)

No, the listeners you have to worry about are the innocuous-looking
subscribers that might in fact be taps into the more secretive parts of
the government. If someone wanted to listen in, a very easy way to do
it would be to put a machine in the US domain, set up an account or
two, and send a subscription request.

So, deliberately excluding the White House from our discussions will
probably not measurably improve security for the subscribers. The only
thing that would give the government pause, I suspect, would be to
convert the list to RSA encryption, although the fine details of that
process lie unfortunately outside my expertise.

On the other hand, at present I doubt that any government agency
looking in would regard us as anything more than harmless cranks. Were
our ideas showing ANY indication of catching on with the public at
large this might change, but the people in power now are complacent and
have every reason to continue to be so.

