Date: Thu, 4 Mar 93 14:18:21 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: POLI: "Fairness"

   From: "W. Scott Meeks" <meeks@osf.org>

   [...]  Fair(1) I take to mean something more like "consistent with
   rules and/or free from improper influence".  This would seem to
   make fair(1) more synonymous with utilitarian(1).  (I assume here
   some function of a linear combination of individual utility
   functions.  A separate argument can be made as to whether the
   global utility function should be some more complex function of the
   individual functions.)

   I would argue that fairness(1) is actually one of the goals of
   libertarian social organization (at least of utilitarian
   libertarianism) and that free markets are in many cases the most
   fair(1) way of performing societal functions such as distribution
   of resources.

But that simply makes me a slave to the total utility of the society,
which is non-LC.  Taking the supermarket example, a "position in line"
opened up; it was not owned by anybody.  Then, in a way that is
perfectly consonant with a free market, the person who was the
quickest to see the opportunity and take action based on it, seized
the position, thus obtaining the benefit of possessing it for himself.

In order to break this argument, you have to argue that the people in
line had an (implicit or explicit) contract to queue in certain ways,
and to allocate newly opening lines in a certain way.  I suspect that
the person in question would never have consented to such a scheme.

This probably sounds quite silly, but it is really a microcosm of all
sorts of "managed competition" schemes in economics.  Consider the
Japanese economy -- the government allocates market opportunities and
burdens in order to provide "fair" competition.  Yuck.

Dale

